    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Puts Snow Tracks on Five SUVs and Trucks, Unloads Them at SEMA 2026 as the Snowhunter Bunch\nBYD's $3 Million Hypercar Crashes at 224 MPH, Driver Walks Away\n24 photos\nPhoto: CarNewsChina | YouTube\nA BYD Yangwang U9 Xtreme, valued at roughly $3 million, violently crashed during a high-speed testing session in China. The test was supposed to precede an intended open-road record attempt, targeting the 310 mph (500 kph) barrier. The automaker will most likely cancel the attempt that was set to take place in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates.\nCrowned the fastest EV at Nurburgring last year, The vehicle was undergoing preliminary high-speed testing at an undisclosed track in China that BYD was using to push the car into the 261 - 280 mph (420 - 450 kph) phase before shipping it to the UAE for the official 311 mph (500 kph) attempt.\nBYD's test driver Jiang Wen, a 23-year veteran driver for the company, was behind the wheel. He survived the crash without serious injuries and was seen walking away from the wreckage. He later received a routine hospital checkup.\nThe driver explained that the vehicle lost traction at extreme speed. When he lifted off the accelerator to correct it, he lost the rear end, which began to swerve out of control, hitting the left-side guardrail at around 224 mph (360 kph). It then spun out and slammed into the right-side guardrail.\nPhoto: CarNewsChina | YouTube The $3 million electric hypercar was entirely destroyed as the rear axle detached from the rest of the vehicle. The front end, along with the entire body, is now a pile of metal. However, the carbon fier/silicon carbide-based passenger safety cell held up and prevented cabin intrusion, keeping the driver safe. The battery pack was not punctured, and no fire followed the crash.\nThe U9 Xtreme previously hit an unofficial/certified top speed of 308.4 mph (496.22 kph) at the Automotive Testing Papenburg facility in Germany back in September 2025. The BYD engineers fine-tuned the powertrain and were ready to take it to the next level in the UAE.\nBreaking speed records were what the U9 Xtreme was actually built for. The car features a highly advanced, ultra-high-voltage powertrain built for record-breaking speed. Sitting on a 1,200-volt silicon carbide platform, the hypercar is set in motion by a quad-motor setup, with each motor independently powering a wheel.\nThe system produces a massive 2,977 horsepower (3,018 metric horsepower) and 2,212 pound-feet (3,000 Newton meters) of torque. That makes it rocket from 0 to 62 mph (0 to 100 kph) in under 2.0 seconds and max out at 308.4 mph (496.22 kph) in standard configuration.\nPhoto: CarNewsChina | YouTube The hypercar features an 80-kWh battery pack with a massive 30C discharge rate. BYD has not disclosed the official range of the U9 Xtreme, but explained that the battery can drain entirely in just a few minutes of continuous top-speed running.\nTo keep the model’s exclusivity, BYD will only roll out 30 such units, each starting at $3 million.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBYD U9 Xtreme BYD BYD hypercar BYD U9 Xtreme horsepower BYD U9 Xtreme price BYD U9 Xtreme crash\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT One Owner 2013 Dodge Challenger R/T Classic for Sale in the States, What’s It Worth to You?",
